IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- National Health Act 1953 (Commonwealth of Australia), Part VII (pharmaceutical benefits) -- what the patient meets at the
-- pharmacy: who is entitled to PBS medicines (s 86, s 89), whether a medicine is a pharmaceutical benefit (s 85(2)-(3)),
-- concessional beneficiary and dependant (s 84(1), (4), (7)), the most the pharmacist may charge (s 87(2), (2A), (3); s 87AA),
-- the PBS safety net and its cards (ss 84B, 84C(1AA)-(1D), 84CA, 84J; s 99F), and refunds (s 87A).
--
-- SEAT: the patient (and the family member whose medicines count with theirs). "Can I get my medicine on the PBS? What will
-- I pay at the chemist? When do I reach the safety net, and what do I pay after that? Who in my family counts? Can I get
-- back what I overpaid?" Provisions addressed to the pharmacist, the Minister or the Secretary are encoded as what they mean
-- for the patient.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 157, compilation date 19 September 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 90, 2026), as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C1953A00095.txt; Part VII Division 1 at lines 234-1030, Division 2 from 1031,
-- Division 4A (indexation) at 3630-3747.
--
-- THE CHARGES ARE INDEXED, AND ARE INPUTS. The general patient charge amount ($25.00 as printed, s 84(1)), the concessional
-- beneficiary charge and the general patient reduced charge ($4.60 as printed, ss 87(2), 99F) and the general patient
-- safety net ($1,457.10 as printed, s 99F) are indexed each 1 January under s 99G (with the freezes in s 99G(1A)-(1B)); "this
-- Act has effect as if the indexed amount were substituted" (s 99G(2)). The s 99G(3)-(4) formulas were dropped from the text
-- deposit, so the indexed amounts are not computed: each question takes the amount in force, and the cases use the printed
-- figures, labelled. The concessional beneficiary safety net is worked out: 36 times the concessional beneficiary charge
-- (s 99F). The value of a supply for safety net purposes is prescribed by reg 17A of the National Health (Pharmaceutical
-- Benefits) Regulations 2017, Compilation No. 16 (15 August 2026, register id F2026C00834), deposited at
-- ../../../national-health-pharmaceutical-benefits-regulations-2017/registers/source-bundle/F2017L00313.txt (lines 231-238):
-- brought in for one supply; the year's total is the patient's input.
--
-- READ WITH the Health Insurance Act 1973 baseline (../../../health-insurance-act-1973/encodings/baseline-2026-10/), which
-- decides whether the patient is an "eligible person" (s 86(1)(a) borrows it) and asks "concessional beneficiary" as a fact
-- for the Medicare safety nets; this encoding works that term out. Cross-folder IMPORT is not possible in the stable l4, so
-- the link is in NOTES.md, not in code.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Part VII Divisions 1A beyond the cards' effect, 2 beyond ss 85-89 (approvals of pharmacists and prescribers,
-- prescriber bags, Medicare numbers), 3 to 4D (payments, recovery, prices, supply guarantees, the Remuneration Tribunal, the
-- Pharmacy Authority, cost recovery, export), Division 5; s 100 special arrangements; the out-patient medication
-- determination (s 84BA); Parts I-VI and VIII onwards. Early supply is asked as a fact (the s 84AAA instrument is not
-- deposited).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the patient's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `National Health Act 1953 Part VII -- the patient and the PBS`

§§ `ss 85, 86, 89 -- can I get my medicine on the PBS`

-- s 86(1): a person who "(a) is, or is to be treated as, an eligible person within the meaning of the Health Insurance Act
-- 1973; and (b) is receiving" medical treatment by a medical practitioner, dental treatment by a participating dental
-- practitioner, optometrical treatment by an authorised optometrist, midwifery treatment by an authorised midwife, nurse
-- practitioner treatment by an authorised nurse practitioner, or nursing treatment by an authorised nurse prescriber "is
-- entitled to receive pharmaceutical benefits ... without the payment ... other than a charge made in accordance with section
-- 87". (2)-(4): a Commonwealth, State or Territory officer working outside Australia, and an accompanying spouse or dependent
-- child, is taken to reside in Australia. s 85(2): the drugs to which Part VII applies are those the Minister declares; s 85(3)
-- the forms. s 89: only if supplied by an approved pharmacist on a PBS prescriber's prescription (or under ss 89A, 92-94).
-- s 86A(1): not supplied if the supplier "has reason to believe that the person is not in Australia" (officers excepted).
DECLARE `Can I get it on the PBS` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I am entitled to receive it as a pharmaceutical benefit, paying no more than the charge s 87 allows (ss 85, 86(1), 89)`
    `no: I am not an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973, nor treated as one (s 86(1)(a))`
    `no: it must be for treatment I am receiving from a medical practitioner, or a participating dentist, or an authorised optometrist, midwife, nurse practitioner or nurse prescriber (s 86(1)(b))`
    `no: the medicine, in this form, is not a drug the Minister has declared for the PBS (s 85(2)-(3))`
    `no: a pharmaceutical benefit must be supplied by an approved pharmacist on a PBS prescriber's prescription, or another way s 89 allows (s 89)`
    `no: the supplier must not supply it, having reason to believe I am not in Australia (s 86A(1))`

@export Can I get this medicine on the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme?
GIVEN `I am an eligible person for Medicare` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973 (for example an Australian resident), or are treated as residing in Australia because you, or the person you are accompanying, work overseas as a Commonwealth, State or Territory officer
      `it is for treatment by a doctor or other PBS prescriber` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are receiving treatment from a medical practitioner, or from a participating dental practitioner, or an authorised optometrist, midwife, nurse practitioner or nurse prescriber, and the medicine is for it
      `the medicine is listed on the PBS in this form` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the drug is one the Minister has declared for the PBS (listed on the Schedule of Pharmaceutical Benefits) in the form prescribed
      `it is supplied by an approved pharmacist on a PBS prescription` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is supplied by an approved pharmacist on a prescription written by a PBS prescriber (or by an approved doctor or hospital, or otherwise as s 89 allows)
      `the pharmacist believes I am overseas` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the pharmacist or other supplier has reason to believe you are not in Australia at the time of supply (does not apply to an officer working overseas, or their accompanying family)
GIVETH A `Can I get it on the PBS`
`Can I get this medicine on the PBS` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I am an eligible person for Medicare`)
    THEN `no: I am not an eligible person under the Health Insurance Act 1973, nor treated as one (s 86(1)(a))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `it is for treatment by a doctor or other PBS prescriber`)
         THEN `no: it must be for treatment I am receiving from a medical practitioner, or a participating dentist, or an authorised optometrist, midwife, nurse practitioner or nurse prescriber (s 86(1)(b))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the medicine is listed on the PBS in this form`)
              THEN `no: the medicine, in this form, is not a drug the Minister has declared for the PBS (s 85(2)-(3))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `it is supplied by an approved pharmacist on a PBS prescription`)
                   THEN `no: a pharmaceutical benefit must be supplied by an approved pharmacist on a PBS prescriber's prescription, or another way s 89 allows (s 89)`
                   ELSE IF `the pharmacist believes I am overseas`
                        THEN `no: the supplier must not supply it, having reason to believe I am not in Australia (s 86A(1))`
                        ELSE `yes: I am entitled to receive it as a pharmaceutical benefit, paying no more than the charge s 87 allows (ss 85, 86(1), 89)`

§§ `s 84(1), (4), (7) -- am I a concessional beneficiary`

-- s 84(1) "concessional beneficiary means: (a) a person who is the holder of a pensioner concession card, a seniors health
-- card or a health care card under the Social Security Act 1991; or (b) a person (other than the holder of the card) whose name
-- is included in a card referred to in paragraph (a); or (c) a person" who is an Australian resident and eligible for fringe
-- benefits under s 53A of the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986; "(d)" an Australian resident eligible for treatment under
-- s 86(1)-(3) of that Act; "(da)" an Australian resident entitled to treatment under s 284 of the Military Rehabilitation and
-- Compensation Act 2004; "(e)" an Australian resident holding a Veterans' Entitlements Act seniors health card.
-- "general patient means a person who is an eligible person within the meaning of the Health Insurance Act 1973, but who is
-- not a concessional beneficiary."
@export Am I a concessional beneficiary for the PBS (paying the concessional charge)?
GIVEN `I hold or am named on a Centrelink concession card` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you hold, or your name is included on, a pensioner concession card, a seniors health card or a health care card under the Social Security Act 1991
      `I am an Australian resident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an Australian resident within the meaning of the Health Insurance Act 1973
      `I have a veterans' entitlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are eligible for fringe benefits under s 53A, or for treatment under s 86(1)-(3), of the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986, or entitled to treatment under s 284 of the Military Rehabilitation and Compensation Act 2004, or hold a Veterans' Entitlements Act seniors health card
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a concessional beneficiary` IF
        `I hold or am named on a Centrelink concession card`
    OR  (`I am an Australian resident` AND `I have a veterans' entitlement`)

-- s 84(4): "A dependant, in relation to a person to whom paragraph (c) or (d) of the definition of concessional beneficiary
-- applies, is a person who is an Australian resident ... and: (a) the spouse of the person; or (b) a child under the age of
-- 16 years who is in the custody, care and control of the person or the spouse of the person; or (c) a person who: (i) has
-- attained the age of 16 years but is under the age of 25 years; and (ii) is receiving full time education at a school,
-- college or university; and (iii) is not being paid a disability support pension ...; and (iv) is wholly or substantially
-- dependent on the person or on the spouse". (7): no dependant of a person who is a concessional beneficiary only because of
-- paragraph (e). Paragraph (da) is not in (4), so its holder has no dependants under it. A dependant pays as a concessional
-- beneficiary does (s 87(2)(a), via "concessional benefit prescription").
DECLARE `How I am related to the veteran` IS ONE OF
    `the veteran's spouse`
    `a child under 16 in the custody, care and control of the veteran or the veteran's spouse`
    `a full-time student aged 16 to 24, wholly or substantially dependent on the veteran or the veteran's spouse, not paid a disability support pension`
    `none of these`

@export Am I the dependant of a veteran, so that I pay the PBS concessional charge too?
GIVEN `I am an Australian resident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an Australian resident within the meaning of the Health Insurance Act 1973
      `how I am related to the veteran` IS A `How I am related to the veteran` @desc How you are related to the veteran
      `the veteran has DVA fringe benefits or treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the veteran is eligible for fringe benefits under s 53A, or for treatment under s 86(1)-(3), of the Veterans' Entitlements Act 1986 (not only a seniors health card, and not only Military Rehabilitation and Compensation Act treatment)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I the dependant of a veteran for the PBS` IF
        `I am an Australian resident`
    AND `the veteran has DVA fringe benefits or treatment`
    AND (NOT (`how I am related to the veteran` EQUALS `none of these`))

§§ `s 87 -- what the pharmacist may charge me`

-- s 87(1): an approved supplier "shall not demand or receive a payment ... in respect of the supply of a pharmaceutical
-- benefit" except as s 87 allows. (2)(a): on a concessional benefit prescription, an entitlement card prescription for an
-- early supply, or a concession card prescription (not an early supply): the concessional beneficiary charge ($4.60 as
-- printed, "less any allowable discount"). (2)(b): on a general benefit prescription where, during the year, the person "is
-- eligible to be issued with a concession card": the general patient reduced charge ($4.60 as printed). (2)(e): otherwise on
-- a general benefit prescription, or a concession card early supply: "the general patient charge amount" or, if "eligible for
-- increased discounting", less. An entitlement card prescription that is not an early supply falls in no paragraph of (2), so
-- nothing may be charged under (2). (2A): plus the special patient contribution (a brand premium), unless the Commonwealth
-- pays it. (3): where the prescriber directs the whole quantity at once (s 88(6)), the charge times the number of supplies it
-- replaces. (4)(b), (6): delivery and after-hours charges are outside this figure.
DECLARE `My PBS status` IS ONE OF
    `I hold a safety net entitlement card`
    `I hold a safety net concession card`
    `I am a concessional beneficiary, or a veteran's dependant, without a safety net card`
    `I am a general patient who has reached the safety net this year but has no card`
    `I am a general patient below the safety net`

GIVEN `status` IS A `My PBS status`
      `early supply` IS A BOOLEAN
      `general charge` IS A NUMBER
      `reduced charge` IS A NUMBER
      `concessional charge` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 87(2) charge` MEANS
    CONSIDER `status`
    WHEN `I hold a safety net entitlement card` THEN
        IF `early supply` THEN `concessional charge` ELSE 0
    WHEN `I hold a safety net concession card` THEN
        IF `early supply` THEN `general charge` ELSE `concessional charge`
    WHEN `I am a concessional beneficiary, or a veteran's dependant, without a safety net card` THEN `concessional charge`
    WHEN `I am a general patient who has reached the safety net this year but has no card` THEN
        IF `early supply` THEN `general charge` ELSE `reduced charge`
    WHEN `I am a general patient below the safety net` THEN `general charge`

@export What is the most the pharmacist may charge me for this PBS medicine?
GIVEN `my PBS status` IS A `My PBS status` @desc Your position for the PBS this calendar year: whether you hold a safety net card, are a concessional beneficiary, or are a general patient below or above the safety net
      `it is an early supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is an early supply: a medicine the Minister has specified, supplied again within the specified period after the last supply of it to you (not on a hospital prescription)
      `the general patient charge in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The general patient charge amount in force at the time of supply, as indexed ($25.00 as printed in s 84(1))
      `the reduced charge in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The general patient reduced charge in force, as indexed ($4.60 as printed in s 87(2)(b))
      `the concessional charge in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The concessional beneficiary charge in force, as indexed ($4.60 as printed in s 87(2)(a))
      `the brand premium` IS A NUMBER @desc The special patient contribution for the brand supplied (a brand price premium), if the pharmacist may charge it; 0 if none
      `the number of supplies combined` IS A NUMBER @desc If the prescriber directed the whole quantity to be supplied at once instead of as repeats, the number of supplies it replaces; otherwise 1
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The most the pharmacist may charge me` MEANS
    (`the s 87(2) charge` `my PBS status` `it is an early supply` `the general patient charge in force` `the reduced charge in force` `the concessional charge in force` TIMES `the number of supplies combined`) PLUS `the brand premium`

-- s 87AA: a supply is "eligible for increased discounting" if it is on a general benefit prescription (not one where the
-- person is eligible for a concession card), not an early supply, and the Commonwealth price is "(i) greater than the general
-- patient charge amount; and (ii) less than or equal to $42.50" (indexed, s 99G). Then (s 87(2)(e)(ii)) the pharmacist may
-- charge "an amount that is less than the general patient charge amount, but greater than nil".
@export May the pharmacist charge me less than the general patient charge for this medicine (increased discounting)?
GIVEN `my PBS status` IS A `My PBS status` @desc Your position for the PBS this calendar year
      `it is an early supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is an early supply of a medicine the Minister has specified
      `the Commonwealth price of the medicine` IS A NUMBER @desc The Commonwealth price for this pharmaceutical benefit (the dispensed price the PBS sets)
      `the general patient charge in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The general patient charge amount in force, as indexed ($25.00 as printed)
      `the increased discounting ceiling in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The upper Commonwealth price for increased discounting in force, as indexed ($42.50 as printed in s 87AA(c)(ii))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the pharmacist discount below the general charge` IF
        `my PBS status` EQUALS `I am a general patient below the safety net`
    AND (NOT `it is an early supply`)
    AND `the Commonwealth price of the medicine` GREATER THAN `the general patient charge in force`
    AND `the Commonwealth price of the medicine` AT MOST `the increased discounting ceiling in force`

§§ `ss 84B, 84C, 84CA, 84J, 99F -- the PBS safety net`

-- s 84C(1AA): a person who has been a general patient during the year "is eligible to be issued with a concession card ... if
-- the total value for safety net purposes of supplies ... made: (a) to the person during the period; or (b) to the person and
-- the person's family during the period; is not less than the amount of the general patient safety net". (1B): eligible "at the
-- time of the supply ... if including the value for safety net purposes of the supply in the total ... would fulfil" it.
-- (1C)-(1D): a concessional beneficiary is eligible for an entitlement card if the total of the values while a concessional
-- beneficiary and the transferred value (s 84CA: each supply at its value or the concessional charge, whichever is less) of
-- supplies while a general patient "is not less than the amount of the concessional beneficiary safety net" (s 99F: 36 times
-- the concessional beneficiary charge). "relevant entitlement period" (s 84(1)): from 1992, each calendar year. Values come
-- from the regulations (s 84C(1E)) and are an input.
DECLARE `Where I am on the safety net` IS ONE OF
    `I have reached it: I am eligible for a safety net concession card, and pay the reduced charge for the rest of the year (ss 84C(1AA), (1B), 87(2)(b))`
    `I have reached it: I am eligible for a safety net entitlement card, and pay nothing more for PBS medicines for the rest of the year apart from early supplies and brand premiums (ss 84C(1C), (1D), 87(2))`
    `not yet` HAS `still to go` IS A NUMBER

@export Have I (or my family) reached the PBS safety net this year?
GIVEN `I am a concessional beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a concessional beneficiary (a Centrelink concession card holder or named on one, or a veteran with a qualifying entitlement), or a veteran's dependant
      `the safety net value of this year's supplies` IS A NUMBER @desc The total value for safety net purposes (as recorded on your prescription record form) of PBS and repatriation medicines and out-patient medication supplied this calendar year to you and your family, including today's supply; for a concessional beneficiary, only those supplied while you were one
      `the transferred value of supplies while a general patient` IS A NUMBER @desc For a concessional beneficiary who was a general patient earlier this year: the supplies made then, each counted at its safety net value or the concessional charge, whichever is less; otherwise 0
      `the general patient safety net in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The general patient safety net in force for this year, as indexed ($1,457.10 as printed in s 99F)
      `the concessional charge in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The concessional beneficiary charge in force, as indexed ($4.60 as printed); the concessional safety net is 36 times it
GIVETH A `Where I am on the safety net`
`Have I reached the PBS safety net` MEANS
    IF `I am a concessional beneficiary`
    THEN IF `the concessional total` AT LEAST `the concessional safety net`
         THEN `I have reached it: I am eligible for a safety net entitlement card, and pay nothing more for PBS medicines for the rest of the year apart from early supplies and brand premiums (ss 84C(1C), (1D), 87(2))`
         ELSE `not yet` (`the concessional safety net` MINUS `the concessional total`)
    ELSE IF `the safety net value of this year's supplies` AT LEAST `the general patient safety net in force`
         THEN `I have reached it: I am eligible for a safety net concession card, and pay the reduced charge for the rest of the year (ss 84C(1AA), (1B), 87(2)(b))`
         ELSE `not yet` (`the general patient safety net in force` MINUS `the safety net value of this year's supplies`)
    WHERE
        `the concessional safety net` MEANS 36 TIMES `the concessional charge in force`
        `the concessional total` MEANS `the safety net value of this year's supplies` PLUS `the transferred value of supplies while a general patient`

-- Regulations reg 17A(3): "the value for safety net purposes is the amount charged under whichever of paragraphs 87(2)(a),
-- (b) and (e) of the Act applies (not including any amount charged under other subsections of section 87)" -- so not a brand
-- premium (s 87(2A)), nor delivery or after-hours charges; (4): "If the amount charged for the supply is not in accordance
-- with the Act, the value for safety net purposes is zero." Reg 17A(2) (supplies to which s 99(2A), (2AB) or (2B) applies:
-- private hospital and similar supplies) is not encoded: those answer NOTHING.
@export How much of what I paid for this PBS supply counts towards my safety net?
GIVEN `what I was charged under the co-payment rules` IS A NUMBER @desc What you were charged as the s 87(2) co-payment for the supply (after any discount), leaving out any brand premium, delivery or after-hours charge
      `the charge was lawful` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount charged for the supply was in accordance with the Act (no more than s 87 allows)
      `it was a private hospital or similar supply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the supply was one to which s 99(2A), (2AB) or (2B) applies (for example, a supply to a patient of a private hospital priced under a s 84C(7) determination)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much of this supply counts towards my safety net` MEANS
    IF `it was a private hospital or similar supply`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `the charge was lawful`
         THEN JUST `what I was charged under the co-payment rules`
         ELSE JUST 0

-- s 84CA: "(a) if the value for safety net purposes of the supply is less than $4.60--that lesser amount; and (b) in any other
-- case--$4.60" (indexed with the concessional beneficiary charge, s 99F).
@export How much does a supply I had as a general patient count towards my concessional safety net?
GIVEN `its safety net value` IS A NUMBER @desc The value for safety net purposes of the supply made while you were a general patient
      `the concessional charge in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The concessional beneficiary charge in force, as indexed ($4.60 as printed)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The transferred value of a supply` MEANS
    IF `its safety net value` LESS THAN `the concessional charge in force` THEN `its safety net value` ELSE `the concessional charge in force`

-- s 84B(1): "the members of a person's family" are "(a) the person's spouse; (b) any dependent child of the person or the
-- person's spouse". (4): "dependent child" is a child under 16 in the person's custody, care and control (or, where no one
-- else has it, wholly or substantially in their care and control), or a "student child" (16 to 24, in full-time education at a
-- school, college or university) "wholly or substantially dependent on the person". "spouse": legally married or de facto,
-- "not living, on a permanent basis, separately and apart" (apart only because of illness or infirmity does not count, (5)).
-- (2): a dependent child at any time in the year stays one for the rest of it.
DECLARE `Who they are to me` IS ONE OF
    `my spouse or de facto partner, living with me (or apart only because of illness or infirmity)`
    `my spouse or de facto partner, living permanently separately and apart from me`
    `a child under 16 in my or my spouse's custody, care and control`
    `a full-time student aged 16 to 24, wholly or substantially dependent on me or my spouse`
    `someone else`

@export Do this person's PBS medicines count towards my family's safety net?
GIVEN `who they are to me` IS A `Who they are to me` @desc Who the person is to you (if a child was your dependent child at any time this year, they count for the rest of the year)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do their medicines count towards my safety net` IF
    CONSIDER `who they are to me`
    WHEN `my spouse or de facto partner, living permanently separately and apart from me` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `someone else` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 84J: a concession card or entitlement card "commences to have effect on the day on which it is issued and ceases to have
-- effect at the end of that period" -- the calendar year.
@export Until when does my PBS safety net card last?
GIVEN `the day the card was issued` IS A DATE @desc The day your safety net concession card or entitlement card was issued
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day my safety net card has effect` MEANS Date 31 12 (DATE_YEAR `the day the card was issued`)

§§ `s 87A -- getting back what I overpaid`

-- s 87A(1)-(2): where the supplier did not supply on concessional or card terms "because the supplier was not satisfied that
-- the person was entitled", and "the Secretary is satisfied that the person was entitled at the time", the person is
-- entitled to the difference. (3)-(4): where the person was eligible for a card but did not hold one, and the Secretary is
-- satisfied "that the failure to issue a ... card was not caused by some wilful action of the person" and the person should
-- be treated as a holder, the person is entitled to "any amount paid by the person that would not have been payable".
-- The Secretary's satisfaction is asked as a fact.
@export How much can I get back from the Commonwealth for a PBS medicine I paid too much for?
GIVEN `what I paid` IS A NUMBER @desc What you were charged for the supply under s 87
      `what I would have paid on the right terms` IS A NUMBER @desc What you would have been charged as the card holder or concessional beneficiary you were entitled to be treated as
      `the Secretary is satisfied I was entitled to those terms` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Secretary is satisfied you were entitled at the time to be supplied on those terms (and, if you had no card, that this was not caused by any wilful act of yours and you should be treated as a card holder)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`The refund I can get` MEANS
    IF `the Secretary is satisfied I was entitled to those terms` AND `what I paid` GREATER THAN `what I would have paid on the right terms`
    THEN `what I paid` MINUS `what I would have paid on the right terms`
    ELSE 0
